{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Living docs still need a reason to get looked at, and kept honest</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Storybook can't drift on prop shape, but a story someone wrote once and never revisited can still describe outdated usage guidance in its own text (the "docs" markdown blocks Storybook itself supports alongside a story). The prop controls stay accurate automatically, the prose around them still needs the same care any documentation does.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Require a story per exported component in CI (a lint rule, or a script diffing exported component names against existing story files) so new components can't ship silently undocumented, this closes the same "we'll add it later" gap that let the old markdown site fall behind in the first place, just enforced automatically instead of by memory.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Treat Storybook's build as a real CI artifact, not just a local dev tool, publish it per-PR (Chromatic and similar services do this) so reviewers see the actual live docs diff for a prop change in the same review, and consider visual regression testing against stories as a byproduct, since every story is already a rendered instance of every documented variant, it's a natural place to also catch unintended visual changes.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

